                    {
                        "sense_id": "b341b3ef-15f8-3f70-9e4d-de090bd6246b",
                        "entry_id": "7990c496-89e8-3d10-ab01-520aad55d7fd",
                        "glosses": [
                            "A homogeneous polynomial in two or more variables."
                        ],
                        "tags": [],
                        "examples": [
                            {
                                "example_id": "4df46e33-d368-49ca-83a5-4e8be7fe27f0",
                                "sense_id": "b341b3ef-15f8-3f70-9e4d-de090bd6246b",
                                "text": "When the two quantics are the first derived functions of the same quantic of any odd order, the lineo-linear invariant does not vanish, but it is not an invariant of the single quantic."
                            },
                            {
                                "example_id": "dd4629d8-7a87-4b94-8b40-f542f40879f4",
                                "sense_id": "b341b3ef-15f8-3f70-9e4d-de090bd6246b",
                                "text": "74. The discriminant of a binary quantic, or the eliminant of a system of binary quantics, is an invariant.\nWe can see a priori that this must be the case, for if a given quantic has a square factor, it will have a square factor still when it is linearly transformed; or if a system of quantics have a common factor, they will still have a common factor when the equations are transformed."
                            }
                        ]
                    }
                ],
